  "floors": [
    "Page faults on a mapped file: ~15 ms per GiB, serialized in the kernel (shared by every tool).",
    "All-core CPU ceiling near 38 GB/s for BLAKE3 on this chip (Rust and Bend both reach it).",
    "macOS open(): ~48 ms per 10,000 files at best, however many threads (volume-wide serialization).",
    "GPU start-up: the Metal heap costs ~50 ms per GiB to set up, so one-shot CLI runs stay on the CPU.",
    "Uncached files: the SSD reads 10-14 GB/s with large parallel preads but 0.2-0.8 GB/s through page faults, so mapping a file that is not in the page cache throws most of the SSD away (Bend streams those).",
    "NVIDIA GPUs: Bend 2.0.39's CUDA runtime hangs inside Modal's sandbox, even for the guide's pow2! example, while plain CUDA managed memory works there; NVIDIA numbers wait on that.",
    "GPU reads: ~5.5-6.8 ms per GiB natively; BLAKE3's compressions fit under them, natively and in Bend, so the GPU hash is read-bound."
  ],
  "proofs": {
    "seconds": 229,
    "note": "bend/prove.sh (bend PROOF.bend with a deep stack), one run on the M5 Pro under outside load: ALL PROOFS CHECK for 28 laws and 45 lemmas. The known answers take nearly all of it; the other laws check in ~36 s."
  },
  "techniques": [
    {
      "adr": "0001",
      "title": "BLAKE3 core in Bend2",
      "summary": "Unboxed U32 records compile to register code; Array<U32> is a packed buffer; Array.fork shares input down a parallel fork tree."
    },
    {
      "adr": "0002",
      "title": "8-lane compress8 + clang SLP",
      "summary": "Eight chunks per call, word-major lanes, one call site, SLP and inline flags: 2.95 GB/s per core vs NEON's 2.40."
    },
    {
      "adr": "0003",
      "title": "b3sum I/O path",
      "summary": "Parallel one-pass loader, zero-copy read-only mmap, byte-balanced file tree, one write for all output."
    },
    {
      "adr": "0004",
      "title": "Files past 4 GiB",
      "summary": "1 GiB segments are complete BLAKE3 subtrees, hashed in parallel and merged as parents (past 4 TiB since ADR 0013)."
    },
    {
      "adr": "0005",
      "title": "Flat GPU leaves",
      "summary": "Fork-free 4/16/64-chunk leaves sized to the 16,384-lane cube: 93-110 GB/s on the GPU."
    },
    {
      "adr": "0006",
      "title": "Native GPU references",
      "summary": "Metal and CUDA BLAKE3 as yardsticks: native Metal hashes 1 GiB in ~6 ms. Bend's GPU path is read-bound: ~1.5x from its lane cube's memory locality, ~1.3x from its array reads."
    },
    {
      "adr": "0007",
      "title": "Laws and proofs",
      "summary": "20 laws the checker proves: official vectors computed by the checker, compress8 and par8 equal the scalar path lane by lane for every input, and BLAKE3's tree splits into 2^k segments and builds level by level, for any compression."
    },
    {
      "adr": "0008",
      "title": "Daemon race, map zone, iroh outboards",
      "summary": "Mapped files go into an address-space zone of their own (one munmap a round); the CPU tree also emits iroh-blobs' bao outboard, byte-identical to bao-tree."
    },
    {
      "adr": "0009",
      "title": "Streaming and a GPU daemon",
      "summary": "Files not in the page cache stream in 1 GiB segments with 16 F_NOCACHE readers while Bend hashes (101.5 GB in ~7.6 s); a daemon refills reused GPU-heap blocks and hashes in one bang."
    },
    {
      "adr": "0010",
      "title": "More laws: outboards, counts, streaming",
      "summary": "28 laws now: the outboard tree gives the plain hash for every input, the tree over n chunks has n leaves and n - 1 parents, and the streaming merge stack builds BLAKE3's tree, every segment once and in order."
    },
    {
      "adr": "0011",
      "title": "Strided GPU lanes; CPU + GPU on one input",
      "summary": "Past 512 MiB, lane l hashes 16-chunk groups l, l + S, l + 2S, l + 3S, so lanes running together read neighbouring memory: 1 GiB 13.2 -> 9.4 ms on the GPU. split.bend lets the CPU and the GPU hash one input at once by slices (13 of 16 on the GPU: 1 GiB in 8.0 ms, 1.06-1.12x the GPU alone)."
    },
    {
      "adr": "0013",
      "title": "Files past 4 TiB",
      "summary": "Every span carries its chunk counter's high word, down to compress8, so files go past 2^32 chunks: a 4 TiB + 1 GiB file matches the official crate, at no measurable cost."
    }
  ],
